Task: Demographic-change mediators (Persistence Extensions — Phase 4, all 3 tasks)

WHY: Could the climate-shock -> health-cost links be an artifact of population
change — chronically shocked counties losing young residents / aging / shifting
tenure, with cost effects merely reflecting who remains? Phase 4 tests this.

WHAT CHANGED:
- download_county_socioeconomic.R: added an ACS demographics pull (B25003 tenure,
  B01001 age, B07001 geographic mobility) for 2011-2023 ->
  Data/County_Socioeconomic/acs_demographics_raw.csv.
- New process_county_demographics.R: compute_demographic_features() derives
  In_Migration_Rate, Pct_Age_65plus, Pct_Owner_Occupied ->
  Data/intermediate_demographics.rds (41,869 rows, 3,234 counties, 2011-2023;
  NA rates In_Migration 2.4%, age/owner 0%).
- New run_demographic_mediators.R: (1) first stage shocks->demographics; (2)
  mediator decomposition of the headline outcomes base vs +demographics on the
  identical sample. Writes demographic_response_coefs.csv (27) and
  demographic_mediator_decomposition.csv (9) + a fraction-surviving plot.
- state_analysis_summary.md: new section 7.
- New test_demographic_mediators.R (5 tests, all pass).

KEY DECISIONS / DEVIATIONS:
- Named the migration variable In_Migration_Rate, NOT the plan's
  Net_Migration_Rate: ACS geographic-mobility tables observe only in-migrants
  (out-migration is not in the table), so a true net rate is not recoverable.
  Documented in the script header.
- B07401 (in-migration by income) was not needed; B07001 supplies the in-mover
  cells (diff-county-same-state + diff-state + abroad).
- Demographics joined at analysis time (run_demographic_mediators.R) rather than
  baked into create_county_master.R, to avoid a full county-pipeline rebuild.
- ACS 5-year estimates are moving averages -> slow-moving compositional controls;
  the weak first-stage is partly a power statement.

KEY RESULTS (a clean, useful null):
- First stage: only 2 of 27 shock->demographic coefficients significant, both
  tiny (High_CDD lag2 -> Pct_Age_65plus +0.0017 p=0.004; High_CDD ->
  Pct_Owner_Occupied -0.0020 p=0.044). Shocks barely move demographics.
- Decomposition: shock coefficients on Medical_Debt_Share, Hosp_BadDebt_PerCapita
  and PCPI survive demographic adjustment essentially intact -- fraction
  surviving 0.94-1.04 for debt/hospital. The only material attenuation is the
  already-insignificant PCPI cold effect (0.58).
- Implication: the headline mechanisms (drought->income->debt; cold->utilization)
  are NOT artifacts of population turnover, aging, or tenure. Closes a
  confounding channel (with the ACS-smoothing power caveat).

FILES: Code/download_county_socioeconomic.R, Code/process_county_demographics.R,
